 Abstract
-This study compares ten single filters and five ordered combinations for YOLOv8n ball detection, motivated by 
-uncertain image characteristics in deployment. Group-separated five-fold cross-validation uses 500 images. Gamma 0.3 
-yields the highest clean-image mAP50-95 among single filters at 96.70%, whereas Gaussian Blur (max) performs best 
-under strong Gaussian noise. No combination exceeds its better component in mAP. The strict-accuracy gain of 
-Invert+CLAHE in one seed is not retained across repeated runs. Neither arbitrary filtering nor composition 
-consistently improves detection. Filter selection must therefore account for the input condition and evaluation metric.
+Ten single filters and five ordered combinations were compared for YOLOv8n ball detection. Five-fold cross-valida
+tion used 500 images with group-separated outer evaluation folds and condition-specific retraining. All filters were test
+ed on clean images; Original and single filters were evaluated under Gaussian noise. In one seed, Gamma 0.3 achiev
+ed clean-image mAP50-95 of 96.70%, exceeding Original by 0.17 percentage points. Gaussian Blur (max) ranked high
+est at noise standard deviations of 40 and 80, scoring 88.79% and 82.94%. No combination exceeded its better comp
+onent in clean-image mAP50-95, and the strict-accuracy gain of Invert+CLAHE was inconsistent across repeated runs. 
+Filter selection should therefore consider image conditions and evaluation metrics.
 Key words
 YOLOv8n, image preprocessing, filter combination, object detection
